  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1237 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe remnants of the H5 low is modeled to be a shortwave trough by \nMonday with temperatures and moisture continue to trend higher. \nDaytime highs climb into the 60s to 70s Monday, and 70s to near 80 \nfor lower elevation areas Tuesday. PWs look to climb to the 0.66\" to \n0.75\" range as well with surface Td's in the 40s to low 50s. This \nwill allow for continue scattered showers with perhaps a stray \nisolated thunderstorm each of these days mainly over western and \ncentral NM. \n\nStronger large scale ascent and forcing doesn't begin to truly \nincrease until Wednesday with the deepening of a stout upper low \ndigging down the CA coastline. Thunderstorm chances increase notably \nover western NM Wednesday afternoon as a result. Model guidance \nadvertises the H5 low turning easterly and coming ashore SoCal \nsometime Thursday or Thursday night. This timing also favors the \nrounding of vortmax along its southern to southeasterly flank, \npeaking thunderstorm activity over AZ/NM. Uncertainty regarding the \ntiming of this remains, with higher certainty that there will be \nample moisture and large scale ascent available for this feature to \ngenerate robust thunderstorm activity over western, northern, and \ncentral NM. PW's approaching the 0.90\" to 1.00\"+ range, which would \nset daily records at ABQ, thanks to multiple factors from low return \nflow pushing in moisture from TX and the Gulf as well as a tap of \nEPAC tropical moisture support the risk of robust rainfall producers \nduring this time. The limiting factor for flooding risk will be fast \nstorm speeds and limited instability due to abundant cloud cover. \nThere remains a low marginal risk for excessive rainfall over the \nFour Corners and Navajo Nation area as a result. \n\nThereafter, numerical model guidance diverges considerably between a \nmore progressive pattern with drying and warming conditions and a \nlagging troughing pattern over the western CONUS continuing \nprecipitation chances toward next weekend.",
